WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
<v Maya>Marcus, I have a theory.</v>

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:06.660
<v Maya>Strip away the strategy decks and the executive presence, and your job is basically...</v>

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.260
<v Maya>nagging people.</v>

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:09.540
<v Maya>Nicely.</v>

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.600
<v Marcus>You're not wrong.</v>

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.920
<v Marcus>But here's the reframe: nobody hands you a Gantt chart.</v>

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:18.820
<v Marcus>The job looks like advising and communicating, but underneath, it's delivery.</v>

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.280
<v Marcus>If things don't get done, nothing else you do matters.</v>

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.180
<v Marcus>Most chiefs of staff don't think of themselves as project managers, and that's exactly the problem.</v>

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:33.120
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.080
<v Maya>So make the case.</v>

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.420
<v Maya>Why is project management the thing?</v>

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.840
<v Marcus>Because everything you touch is a project.</v>

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:45.220
<v Marcus>The board deck, the offsite, the reorg, the product launch the CEO cares about.</v>

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.860
<v Marcus>Each one has a scope, a deadline, a dozen stakeholders, a hundred moving pieces.</v>

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.720
<v Marcus>You have to see the project inside the chaos and run it.</v>

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:02.400
<v Marcus>The chiefs of staff who thrive are the ones who instinctively break big ambiguous goals into owned, dated pieces of work.</v>

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.200
<v Maya>What's the visible difference?</v>

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.460
<v Maya>The one with the discipline versus the one without it?</v>

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.000
<v Marcus>Without it, you're a mailbox.</v>

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.900
<v Marcus>Information comes in, you pass it along, you hope things happen.</v>

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.840
<v Marcus>With it, you're the engine.</v>

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.160
<v Marcus>Every commitment out of a meeting gets an owner and a date.</v>

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.620
<v Marcus>Every initiative has a next milestone everyone can see.</v>

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.380
<v Marcus>People start noticing things move faster when you're involved.</v>

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.220
<v Marcus>That's not charisma.</v>

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:28.800
<v Marcus>That's project management.</v>

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.660
<v Marcus>Least visible skill, most felt.</v>

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.160
<v Maya>Okay, so what are the actual tools?</v>

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.620
<v Maya>Someone's never run a project formally.</v>

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.020
<v Maya>Where do they start?</v>

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.540
<v Marcus>Three habits cover eighty percent of it.</v>

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.340
<v Marcus>First, define done.</v>

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.700
<v Marcus>Before anything starts, write down what finished looks like, in one sentence.</v>

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:52.260
<v Marcus>Most misalignment is just two people with different definitions of done.</v>

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.360
<v Marcus>Second, every commitment gets an owner and a date.</v>

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.100
<v Marcus>Not a team, a person.</v>

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.820
<v Marcus>Not soon, a date.</v>

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.840
<v Marcus>If it doesn't have both, it doesn't exist.</v>

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.920
<v Marcus>Third, keep one tracker.</v>

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:12.420
<v Marcus>One list of every open commitment, reviewed every week.</v>

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.540
<v Marcus>Doesn't need fancy software.</v>

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:16.300
<v Marcus>A spreadsheet works.</v>

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.760
<v Marcus>The discipline is the review, not the tool.</v>

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.200
<v Maya>A spreadsheet.</v>

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.160
<v Maya>That's the big secret?</v>

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:24.740
<v Marcus>That's the big secret.</v>

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.220
<v Marcus>It's never the tool.</v>

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.920
<v Marcus>It's the weekly review.</v>

00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.820
<v Maya>I can hear the objection, though.</v>

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:32.420
<v Maya>Doesn't this turn you into a taskmaster?</v>

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.420
<v Maya>The person always nagging for status updates?</v>

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.960
<v Marcus>That's the failure mode, and it's real.</v>

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.040
<v Marcus>The difference is how you drive.</v>

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.680
<v Marcus>A taskmaster demands updates.</v>

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.060
<v Marcus>A good chief of staff makes it easy to deliver.</v>

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:49.500
<v Marcus>You clear the blocker before you ask for the status.</v>

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.780
<v Marcus>You draft the thing so they only have to react.</v>

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.560
<v Marcus>You protect people's time so they want to work with you.</v>

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.640
<v Marcus>Driving without authority is the whole game.</v>

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.220
<v Marcus>Nobody reports to you, so your only leverage is being useful.</v>

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:08.040
<v Marcus>The moment people feel you're just tracking them, you've lost.</v>

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:13.040
<v Marcus>The moment they feel you make their work easier, they'll move mountains for you.</v>

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.440
<v Maya>Hardest version.</v>

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.740
<v Maya>Your principal keeps adding priorities.</v>

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.660
<v Maya>Everything is urgent.</v>

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.520
<v Maya>How do you push back without getting fired?</v>

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.520
<v Marcus>This is where scoping becomes a superpower.</v>

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:27.020
<v Marcus>You never say no to your principal.</v>

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:28.940
<v Marcus>You say: here's the trade.</v>

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:33.980
<v Marcus>If we take on this new priority, here's what slips, and by how long.</v>

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.020
<v Marcus>You make the cost visible.</v>

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:38.980
<v Marcus>Most principals aren't being unreasonable.</v>

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:42.100
<v Marcus>They just can't see the full load the way you can.</v>

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.060
<v Marcus>You're the only person with the complete picture.</v>

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:49.480
<v Marcus>So put the choices in front of them, clearly, no drama.</v>

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.540
<v Marcus>That's not defiance.</v>

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.660
<v Marcus>That's the most valuable service you can offer.</v>

00:03:55.600 --> 00:04:01.600
<v Marcus>A chief of staff who can't do this becomes the person who says yes to everything and delivers nothing.</v>

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:04.220
<v Maya>What's the mistake even good ones make?</v>

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.140
<v Maya>The trap for people who already do this well?</v>

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:09.780
<v Marcus>Tracking everything and driving nothing.</v>

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:16.840
<v Marcus>They build a beautiful tracker, color coded, every commitment logged, and then they just watch it.</v>

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.880
<v Marcus>Here's the uncomfortable truth: a tracker is not a project.</v>

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.140
<v Marcus>It's a museum of intentions.</v>

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:26.220
<v Marcus>The value isn't in recording the work.</v>

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:29.640
<v Marcus>It's in the weekly review where you ask "what's stuck?"</v>

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.600
<v Marcus>and then go unstick it.</v>

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:35.660
<v Marcus>Second mistake: confusing activity with progress.</v>

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:42.540
<v Marcus>Lots of motion, lots of meetings, lots of updates, and the milestone never moves.</v>

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:48.180
<v Marcus>Always ask: did this week move the outcome, or did it just move paper?</v>

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.500
<v Maya>So how does this become a reputation?</v>

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.200
<v Maya>How do you stand out with it?</v>

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:53.980
<v Marcus>Run the operating cadence.</v>

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.580
<v Marcus>The weekly business review, the monthly check-in, the quarterly planning cycle.</v>

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:05.800
<v Marcus>Whoever runs the rhythm of the company becomes indispensable, because the rhythm is what turns strategy into results.</v>

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:14.740
<v Marcus>Be the person who sends the pre-read on time, who ends every meeting with owners and dates, who follows up before anyone has to ask.</v>

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.380
<v Marcus>Do that for six months and you won't need to explain your value.</v>

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:20.480
<v Marcus>Everyone will have felt it.</v>

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:21.660
<v Maya>Let me recap.</v>

00:05:22.300 --> 00:05:25.680
<v Maya>Everything you touch is a project, labeled or not.</v>

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.280
<v Maya>Define done in one sentence.</v>

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.420
<v Maya>Every commitment gets an owner and a date.</v>

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.420
<v Maya>One tracker, reviewed every week.</v>

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.100
<v Maya>Drive by making it easy, not by nagging.</v>

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:42.500
<v Maya>When priorities pile up, show the trade instead of just saying yes.</v>

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:48.240
<v Maya>And run the operating cadence, because whoever runs the rhythm becomes indispensable.</v>

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.580
<v Marcus>And remember the core idea.</v>

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:52.340
<v Marcus>Advice is cheap.</v>

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:53.820
<v Marcus>Delivery is rare.</v>

00:05:54.500 --> 00:06:00.400
<v Marcus>The chief of staff who delivers, consistently, becomes the most trusted person in the building.</v>

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.500
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:06.920
<v Maya>See you next week!</v>

